gif(4): native software TSO via tcp_tso_chop() - the tunnel demo.

The CHOPPER.txt P5 demo adopter: gif advertises IFCAP_TSO4/6
(default-off, like if_pair(4) and epair(4)), maps the grant into
if_hwassist, and inner-chops accepted chains in gif_transmit()
BEFORE encapsulation - each segment then encapsulates exactly like
an ordinary packet, so in_gif_output()/in6_gif_output() and the
outer path never see an oversized frame.  gif advertises no TXCSUM,
so the chopper runs with flags 0 (full software checksums, matching
what tcp_output() would have produced without the grant).  The
sender-side win is the usual software-TSO one: TCP builds few large
chains instead of many wire-size packets, saving per-packet
stack traversals down to the tunnel.

Requires the tcp_tso_chop() KPI in the kernel
(software-tso-forwarding.patch + tcp_tso.{c,h}).

Structure: the old gif_transmit() body from the af extraction down
becomes gif_transmit1(ifp, m, af); gif_transmit() keeps the entry
checks and adds the chop loop.  Note gif_output()'s csum_data "af
cheat" - the af is extracted before the chopper runs, and travels
as a parameter, because the chopper's segments carry fresh pkthdrs.

Apply from /usr/src:  patch -p0 < gif-tso.patch

